# Hardware smoke checks

There are no automated runtime tests yet. GitHub Actions checks that the root
NanoC6 firmware compiles with include/secrets.example.h.

After building and uploading with PlatformIO:

1. Connect ENV III to the NanoC6 Grove port. Open the serial monitor at 115200 baud
   and verify sensor detection, LittleFS initialization, and live readings every 5 s.
2. With upstream Wi-Fi credentials blank, connect to MUSHROOM-ENV and open
   http://192.168.4.1/. Verify temperature, humidity, pressure, and dew point update.
3. Verify the first sample is logged, then wait at least 5 minutes and download
   CSV. Check the header, additional aggregate row, sample count, and min/max values.
4. Use browser clock sync and verify new CSV rows contain UTC timestamps.
5. Reboot. Verify previous records remain and new records use a new session ID.
6. Disconnect the browser, leave the logger running for over 5 minutes, reconnect,
   and confirm logging continued without a client.
7. If using upstream Wi-Fi, verify LAN access and NTP synchronization with internet
   access. Disconnect the router and confirm the direct AP still works.
8. On a device containing only disposable test data, use Clear logs and verify
   old records disappear and new records are subsequently written.

Long-duration validation also needs to confirm that storage rotation deletes the
oldest chunks while keeping newer data. Compilation alone does not establish this.
